WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.220
There are few studios that I feel encapsulate that pure passion for an art form.

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:09.660
One where no matter what project they take on,

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.220
you know it will be given the same care and attention as when they first started,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:18.900
where every frame exudes that same joyous energy that reminds you

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:22.480
why you fell in love with this art form in the first place.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:28.500
And in anime for me, one of those studios was

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.160
Kyoto Animation

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.560
I remember first hearing about KyoAni when I watched Full Metal Panic? Fumoffu, and The Second Raid,

00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:39.380
and this was before my young brain really comprehended

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.140
that different shows were made by different studios.

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:48.080
But I noticed that these two seasons just looked vastly better than the first

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:51.260
and I wanted to know what the reason for that was

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:56.100
and it turned out that it was because a new studio was in charge of these new seasons,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:58.380
Kyoto Animation

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.480
Their mark of quality was what made me strive to find them out.

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.060
They were the first studio I knew by name because of this and even though

00:01:07.060 --> 00:01:10.740
this is vastly different from the types of shows they're known for now,

00:01:10.740 --> 00:01:17.200
one thing that has stayed consistent in all these years is that same drive for quality.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.040
I'm not going to pretend that I've seen all lights everything they've made

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:28.080
but it's impossible to deny that their work is imbued with a certain magic

00:01:28.080 --> 00:01:32.800
and I think that's why they're so loved and respected by many of us in this community.

00:01:33.500 --> 00:01:39.100
I can't count the number of conversations I've had with people who couldn't stop crying during Clannad,

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:43.540
who had Nichijou to thank for gut-busting laughter after gut-busting laughter,

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.580
who were taught the value of family with Dragon Maid,

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:50.380
got inspired by Kumiko's journey in Sound! Euphonium,

00:01:50.380 --> 00:01:56.320
and yes, were deeply moved by some cute girls and their motherfucking tea.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:02:01.460
Violet Evergarden last year which I initially thought I didn't like, ended up raising their

00:02:01.460 --> 00:02:07.340
inconceivably high bar of quality even higher, having moments that took my breath away,

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:11.960
And of course not forgetting A Silent Voice which was such a raw,

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:16.400
delicate depictions of the smallest tragedies you find in life.

00:02:16.500 --> 00:02:22.400
Even disregarding the countless tears I shed during this movie, it blows my mind that

00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.200
out of all the epic monologues, witty exchanges and outspoken characters on display elsewhere in anime,

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.900
the one character who could communicate more succinctly than any of that

00:02:32.900 --> 00:02:37.860
was an anime girl who had trouble vocalizing a single word.

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:41.880
That is the magic of Kyoto Animation.

00:02:42.560 --> 00:02:46.620
While writing this, I tried to think of what word encapsulates everything

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:51.000
this studio is about and the best I could come up with was..

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:52.320
Touching

00:02:52.800 --> 00:02:57.020
The studio has this mystic ability to be able to craft these beautiful stories

00:02:57.020 --> 00:03:00.860
that connect with an audience in a way not many others can.

00:03:01.040 --> 00:03:03.480
Sometimes they're cute, sometimes they're funny,

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:07.540
sometimes they can flex amazing action sequences just for the hell of it,

00:03:07.540 --> 00:03:12.860
but peeling back all those layers, these are stories that have touched and moved a whole generation

00:03:12.860 --> 00:03:17.760
of anime fans that couldn't be more thankful to have discovered their work.

00:03:18.220 --> 00:03:23.960
A few months ago, I took to re-watching the entire Haruhi series for absolutely no reason.

00:03:24.220 --> 00:03:27.140
No plan to mention it in the video. No tweet about it.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:33.980
I just wanted to re-experience it for myself and I hope you understand that for someone in my position,

00:03:34.360 --> 00:03:36.320
this is extremely rare.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:39.360
Haruhi changed me as an anime fan.

00:03:39.460 --> 00:03:44.340
As a teenager who was only interested in flashy shown in action or cool mecha battles,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:48.260
this show was so out of left field from anything I thought I'd enjoy

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:51.980
so I watched it with the pretense that I was probably gonna shit on it

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:55.420
as another dumb, over-hyped high school anime.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:04:00.640
And here I am over 10 years later with a massive grin on my face

00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:05.920
that not a single person could wipe off just because I got to hear this track again.

00:04:11.040 --> 00:04:15.160
I can still hear Kyon's monologue everytime this comes on.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:19.140
Even back then I could tell how much passion went into it.

00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:23.080
I never knew some high school anime could look so vibrant and alive.

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:25.860
I slowly started to fall in love with this group of misfits.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:32.340
The school festival episode floored me, sending me down a spiral of trying to find every music anime I could,

00:04:32.340 --> 00:04:37.320
and then there was Kyon, whose snarky wit and sarcastic comments when you know it,

00:04:37.720 --> 00:04:43.040
resonated with a certain other snarky, sarcastic British git watching it.

00:04:43.820 --> 00:04:48.060
But the real turning points was The Disappearance of Haruhi Suzumiya,

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:51.040
which now after having recently re-watched it,

00:04:51.040 --> 00:04:56.860
I can firmly say is still one of my favorite anime films of all time.

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:00.560
It showed me that behind Kyon's snark and sarcasm,

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:05.980
there was just a kid who truly cared, who was scared to lose everything that he took for granted.

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:10.740
His choice is a scene that will forever be ingrained in my memory.

00:05:10.880 --> 00:05:15.380
Because no matter how many wars of cynicism I erect as I grow older,

00:05:15.380 --> 00:05:22.280
there will always be a part of me that still yearns for that same childish joy you could feel when you were a kid.

00:05:23.300 --> 00:05:29.000
This is just one of the stories they've made that has touched me, and I'm sure many of you watching

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:33.920
will have your own stories and memories of how some of their works have affected you in some way.

00:05:34.500 --> 00:05:38.440
People finds different ways to express their passion for something they love

00:05:38.740 --> 00:05:40.700
and this was mine..

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:56.600
I don't play piano, I've never had lessons, I've never owned one, I can't read music sheets,

00:05:56.600 --> 00:05:59.500
and yet one day on my roommates keyboard,

00:05:59.500 --> 00:06:03.320
I decided I wanted to learn this song that was used in Disappearance

00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:07.920
by essentially just watching covers on YouTube and trying the best I could

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:12.740
to fumble my virgin piano hands into copying what I was seeing on screen, and..

00:06:12.820 --> 00:06:17.860
apparently I beat it into myself so deeply that I can still play it today.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:23.200
There wasn't any big reason why I decided to dedicate several of my evenings doing this.

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:30.900
To this day, this is the only song I'm able to play on piano. But for me, this is a mark I'll always have

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:35.340
because those evenings I took to learn a song when an instrument I never played

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:42.760
was my own way of expressing how much I loved this movie and how deeply it affected me.

00:06:46.120 --> 00:06:49.640
I don't even know if the people who made this are alive anymore.

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:56.240
Nothing can prepare you for the complete shock to your system

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:00.860
when you're given the type of horrible news that leaves you stunned and speechless,

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:04.040
and last week, that's what happened.

00:07:04.780 --> 00:07:07.440
34 deaths, 35 injured.

00:07:07.540 --> 00:07:11.200
The worst mass murder to happen in Japan since World War 2

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:16.400
for a studio that only has around 160 employees.

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:22.680
There aren't any words that exist that can encapsulate a tragedy such as this.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:26.260
You see the headlines and try to form the right words to say,

00:07:26.260 --> 00:07:30.960
but nothing that comes up even seems remotely adequate enough.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:38.040
When you hear of a tragedy, you instinctively search for information and details that can help you comprehend it.

00:07:38.300 --> 00:07:40.800
Why did this happen? What was the motive?

00:07:40.800 --> 00:07:45.240
What was the events leading up to this that would cause such a horrific act to happen?

00:07:45.740 --> 00:07:50.320
But it's in that fruitless pursuit you'll come to the same sickening realization

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:55.400
that no matter what information you learn about it, there is no way you can fathom it.

00:07:55.540 --> 00:08:01.140
There is no silver lining, no way to make it better, no closure to be had.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:05.720
It just hurts. It really hurts.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:09.680
I wish there was something we could grasp onto..

00:08:09.720 --> 00:08:12.500
Something that didn't make us feel so helpless

00:08:12.500 --> 00:08:16.060
in the face of what's been taken away and can never be replaced.

00:08:16.840 --> 00:08:20.180
I can't even begin to comprehend the twisted fate

00:08:20.180 --> 00:08:25.540
that would allow such a tragic event to happen to a place that brought so many smiles.

00:08:26.140 --> 00:08:30.720
For our anime community, this is by far the worst thing that's ever happened,

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:35.440
and it's put into perspective how small and connected our community might still be

00:08:35.440 --> 00:08:38.140
when mainstream media outlets are reporting on this,

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:44.160
#PrayForKyoAni is trending worldwide and yet this feels so oddly personal.

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:48.640
If you like to help out, there is a GoFundMe by Sentai Filmworks

00:08:48.640 --> 00:08:50.920
or if you want to support KyoAni directly,

00:08:50.920 --> 00:08:54.600
you can do so by purchasing some of their digital prints on their website.

00:08:54.740 --> 00:08:58.160
Links in the description for all the information you need.

00:08:58.480 --> 00:09:04.420
I will be donating a hundred percent of the first two weeks adsense of this video before turning the ads off

00:09:04.420 --> 00:09:07.080
along with a thousand dollars of my own money.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:10.680
If I was ever thankful I have any kind of reach,

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:13.680
it's so I can help spread this word and even if I've just

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:18.680
convinced the few of you to help out too, it'll all have been completely worth it.

00:09:18.780 --> 00:09:23.640
Normally I try to end these kinds of videos on a hopeful, heartwarming notes

00:09:23.640 --> 00:09:26.840
and though I'm proud of this response the community has given,

00:09:26.840 --> 00:09:31.220
there is nothing that could make up for the devastation that's happened.

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:35.020
So I'll end this the only way I know I can.

00:09:35.700 --> 00:09:43.160
To everyone at Kyoto Animation, to everyone who has managed to put a smile on my face, touched me, moved me in some way

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:51.360
and even though it hurts to say, to everyone who I'll now never be able to express what their work meant to me..

00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:54.400
From the bottom of my heart,

00:09:54.980 --> 00:09:55.940
Thank you.

